WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:12.269
Back in UE5, use the Quickly Add to the Project button on the main toolbar, and import the Datasmith file named Context.udatasmith that's been provided to you.

2
00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:19.230
You are asked for a location on disk, create a folder named Theatron.

3
00:00:21.899 --> 00:00:27.199
When asked for import options, make sure all options are enabled and import the file.

4
00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:39.359
You may get a warning or two. In this case, the warning is about a Twinmotion material using the Grunge feature, which is not compatible with UE5.

5
00:00:40.119 --> 00:00:43.920
Aside from that, everything seems to be properly in place.

6
00:00:46.659 --> 00:00:52.359
Note the presence of multiple Twinmotion cameras that were derived from the Media panel in Twinmotion.

7
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:04.319
Keep in mind that Datasmith doesn't cater for collision data, and LODs behave erratically at times.

8
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.939
We'll look how to deal with that later in this class.

9
00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.739
Let's bring in another part of the project.

10
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.040
Repeat the import process, this time for the vegetation part.

11
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:23.359
When you get to the Import Options dialog, disable cameras as you don't want them duplicated.

12
00:01:25.239 --> 00:01:29.040
Once the Datasmith file is imported, look at the Outliner.

13
00:01:29.599 --> 00:01:35.099
You have the Context and Vegetation folders representing the two Datasmith file hierarchies.

14
00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.269
Both contain a Background entry.

15
00:01:41.349 --> 00:01:46.349
That's a duplicated actor representing the circular city background around the scene.

16
00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.230
You can safely delete one of those.

17
00:01:53.290 --> 00:02:01.590
The other thing you'll notice as you zoom in with the imported trees, is that although they import properly, there is a problem with the display.

18
00:02:02.569 --> 00:02:14.949
This promises to be fixed soon, but for now, the easiest fix is to save your level and all its assets, and restart the project.

19
00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:26.300
The trees look better now, although that last LOD level leaves a lot to be desired.

20
00:02:27.539 --> 00:02:32.340
And as mentioned, Datasmith doesn't carry over any collision information between apps.

21
00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:38.449
Dealing with LODs and collisions one tree type at a time would be too time consuming.

22
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.699
So let's look at an alternative way of importing the data.

23
00:02:43.909 --> 00:02:50.610
To delete all vegetation in the scene and in the project, start by selecting the Vegetation top parent in the Outliner.

24
00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:57.650
Press Ctrl-Shift-D to select the hierarchy underneath and then press Delete.

25
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:08.930
Confirm all deletion and then select the files and folders in the Vegetation folder in the Content Browser and do the same.

26
00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:20.310
Now that all vegetation is removed from the project, we'll import it again using a different technique.

27
00:03:21.389 --> 00:03:29.949
In the empty Vegetation folder in the Content Browser, right-click and create a Dataprep asset. Name it Vegetation.

28
00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:34.189
Double-click the asset to edit it in the Dataprep Editor.

29
00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:40.009
Here, you can decide how to sort and adjust your incoming data in a node-based editor.

30
00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:48.449
Start by selecting the file to import, in this case the Vegetation Datasmith file.

31
00:03:51.449 --> 00:03:53.949
Click the Import button to see the file contents.

32
00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:03.020
Note that the Datasmith file contains duplicates of elements you already have in the scene, such as the background and the cameras.

33
00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:09.439
To get rid of those, you can filter the selection using an actor label.

34
00:04:12.439 --> 00:04:20.290
You can type the name of an actor you wish to select, such as Background, and then add a Delete Objects node to that action.

35
00:04:23.449 --> 00:04:27.449
Add another actor label to the graph, this time looking for cameras.

36
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.980
To select the child actors, add a Select Hierarchy node to the action.

37
00:04:38.689 --> 00:04:43.189
And finally, a Delete Objects node to finish off that process.

38
00:04:45.519 --> 00:04:49.519
To test your work, click the Execute button on the toolbar.

39
00:04:50.439 --> 00:04:55.740
After a moment, you'll notice that the background and the camera actors have disappeared from the outliner.

40
00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.939
Next, consider that all the trees in the incoming file have the label Growth in them.

41
00:05:04.529 --> 00:05:13.319
Add a new actor label node based on the name Growth, and then add a Set LOD node to the action.

42
00:05:16.839 --> 00:05:21.439
Disable Auto-Screen Size if you plan to define how the LODs will transition.

43
00:05:22.639 --> 00:05:27.040
Add three LOD array elements, or three LOD levels if you will.

44
00:05:27.639 --> 00:05:29.439
That should be enough for our purposes.

45
00:05:30.819 --> 00:05:34.920
Adjust the numbers, you can follow my lead or experiment with other numbers.

46
00:05:36.449 --> 00:05:39.350
This will take care of adding LODs for all trees.

47
00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:45.670
Similarly, add a Set Convex Collision to the action.

48
00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.620
Leave the default values as they are.

49
00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:52.759
This will apply collision detection for all trees in the scene.

50
00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:56.689
Execute the recipe to test the results.

51
00:05:57.189 --> 00:06:00.889
This will take a while, but it's still faster than doing the work manually.

52
00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:11.949
Once you're happy, click the Commit button to send the results to the editor.

53
00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:21.860
Once you're back in the editor, save your data using the Save All button.

54
00:06:25.189 --> 00:06:30.689
You still need to restart your project to refresh the texturing, but otherwise, you should be good to go.

55
00:06:36.569 --> 00:06:41.069
As good as visual data prep is, sometimes it may just be overkill.

56
00:06:41.959 --> 00:06:45.160
For the hero building, a simple import will work just fine.

57
00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.959
Import the theory.datasmith file as you have learned earlier.

58
00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:57.560
Again, make sure the Cameras option is disabled to prevent duplicates.

59
00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:05.620
You'd still need to delete the duplicated background that is also part of this datasmith file.

60
00:07:12.519 --> 00:07:18.420
For the most part, everything seems to be working well, but there are adjustments to be made at the lighting level.

61
00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:24.670
All incoming lights are currently set to Static, as evidenced by the red warning in the viewport.

62
00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.360
It suggests that light baking is needed for the scene.

63
00:07:29.149 --> 00:07:34.050
If you're planning to use Lumen instead, all incoming lights should be set to Movable.

64
00:07:35.519 --> 00:07:40.120
Other light issues can be viewed at night. Set the time of day to about 9pm.

65
00:07:47.279 --> 00:07:53.279
Some spotlights could use a burst of energy, but the bigger problem lies in the street lights and bollards.

66
00:07:54.069 --> 00:07:59.470
The lights associated with those should have shadow casting turned off to see any effects.

67
00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:10.970
To fix all these issues, you need a tool to bulk edit lights.

68
00:08:11.959 --> 00:08:14.560
You can use the Light Mixer tool in the Window menu.

69
00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:23.019
The tool lists all the lights in your scene. It can also list properties that you need to edit.

70
00:08:23.519 --> 00:08:27.120
You can add or remove properties by right-clicking the menu bar.

71
00:08:28.459 --> 00:08:31.360
You already have the Mobility property displayed.

72
00:08:32.019 --> 00:08:38.779
Add Cast Shadows, Intensity, and Indirect Lighting intensity to the mix.

73
00:08:43.059 --> 00:08:48.360
Select any of the entries in the Light Mixer and then press Ctrl-A to select all the lights.

74
00:08:49.409 --> 00:08:53.110
You can now easily change their mobility status to Immovable.

75
00:08:54.409 --> 00:09:00.409
The Light Baking Red warning doesn't disappear from the viewport, which needs a refresh. We'll do that in a moment.

76
00:09:02.059 --> 00:09:05.460
Scroll down the list and select Street Lights in the Light Mixer.

77
00:09:07.019 --> 00:09:09.919
Right-click and choose Select Immediate Children.

78
00:09:11.289 --> 00:09:14.690
Do it again on the sub-selection to ensure the whole hierarchy is selected.

79
00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:22.190
Disable Cast Shadows to ensure they're off on street lamps and bollards.

80
00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:26.470
Unfortunately, the effect is not immediate in the viewport.

81
00:09:27.159 --> 00:09:33.480
Save your level and reload it to see the effect.

82
00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:39.820
Note also that the Light Baking message has disappeared.

83
00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:44.019
The Street Lights intensities could use a little boost.

84
00:09:48.490 --> 00:10:00.259
In the Light Mixer, select all Streamlined entries and set the light intensities to 800 all around.

85
00:10:03.419 --> 00:10:07.320
Again, it is unfortunate that you must save and reload the level to see the effects.

86
00:10:07.919 --> 00:10:11.320
Here's hoping that this becomes more interactive in a future release.

87
00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.929
Go back to a time of day around 1pm when you're done.

88
00:10:16.529 --> 00:10:19.629
Next, we'll discuss LODs and collisions.
